WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.260
Configuration parsing should create a structured intermediate representation before applying settings.

2
00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:13.560
That representation might be a map of keys, a list of records, a tree of sections, or a typed configuration object.

3
00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:17.580
The key idea is to understand the structure before changing runtime behavior.

4
00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:20.820
Key-value formats still need precise rules.

5
00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:32.520
The parser should define how keys and values are separated, whether whitespace is trimmed, whether empty values are allowed, how comments are recognized, and whether comments are allowed after values.

6
00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:36.220
Small syntax choices can change what the program believes was configured.

7
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.220
Quoting and escaping rules should be intentional.

8
00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:47.480
If a value can contain a delimiter, newline, backslash, or quote character, the parser needs a deterministic way to represent it.

9
00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:53.980
If the format does not support those cases, reject them clearly instead of accepting ambiguous text.

10
00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:57.860
Structured formats add section and ordering questions.

11
00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:07.400
Duplicate sections, repeated keys inside a section, inherited values, and ordering-dependent behavior should all be covered by the contract.

12
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.900
If ordering matters, document it.

13
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.040
If ordering should not matter, implement and test that expectation.

14
00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:20.600
Ambiguous or incomplete syntax should not drift into defaults or partial state.

15
00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:33.900
A missing delimiter, unclosed quote, malformed section header, truncated record, or unexpected token should produce a clear parse error and avoid applying settings from an uncertain representation.

16
00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:37.300
Separating parse from apply makes this easier.

17
00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:46.360
First load the bytes, then parse the structure, then validate types and values, then apply the resulting configuration only if the whole contract is satisfied.
